1
00:00:06,480 --> 00:00:12,230
A farm has a cylinder-shaped water tank, 1.2 metres across and 1.5 metres tall.

2
00:00:12,280 --> 00:00:16,520
It also has a trough shaped like a trapezium prism.

3
00:00:16,570 --> 00:00:21,720
What is a cylinder: like a prism with a circular cross-section, or a pyramid?

4
00:00:21,770 --> 00:00:24,950
Pause the video and choose.

5
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:28,240
It is like a prism with a circular cross-section.

6
00:00:28,290 --> 00:00:31,340
The circle is the same all the way up.

7
00:00:31,390 --> 00:00:37,560
Remember: the volume of a prism is the area of the cross-section, times the length.

8
00:00:37,610 --> 00:00:44,160
A prism has the same cross-section all along its length: a triangle, a trapezium, a hexagon.

9
00:00:44,210 --> 00:00:51,620
The trough's cross-section is a trapezium with parallel sides 0.5 and 0.3 metres, and height 0.4 metres.

10
00:00:51,670 --> 00:00:53,610
It is 2 metres long.

11
00:00:53,660 --> 00:00:58,060
The area is a half times 0.5 plus 0.3, times 0.4.

12
00:00:58,110 --> 00:01:00,510
That is 0.16 square metres.

13
00:01:00,560 --> 00:01:03,780
1 cubic metre is 1,000 litres.

14
00:01:03,830 --> 00:01:04,460
Your turn.

15
00:01:04,510 --> 00:01:07,440
What is the volume of the trough, in litres?

16
00:01:07,490 --> 00:01:10,680
Pause the video and try it.

17
00:01:10,730 --> 00:01:16,890
0.16 times 2 is 0.32 cubic metres, which is 320 litres.

18
00:01:16,940 --> 00:01:23,700
For a cylinder, the cross-section is a circle, so the volume is pi R squared, times the height.

19
00:01:23,750 --> 00:01:28,430
The tank is 1.2 metres across, so its radius is 0.6 metres.

20
00:01:28,480 --> 00:01:30,980
The height is 1.5 metres.

21
00:01:31,030 --> 00:01:31,680
Your turn.

22
00:01:31,730 --> 00:01:36,200
Find the volume of the tank, in cubic metres, to 1 decimal place.

23
00:01:36,250 --> 00:01:39,130
Pause and work it out.

24
00:01:39,180 --> 00:01:44,210
Pi times 0.36 times 1.5 is about 1.696 cubic metres.

25
00:01:44,260 --> 00:01:48,870
That is about 1.7, or nearly 1,700 litres.

26
00:01:48,920 --> 00:01:50,220
Here is the method.

27
00:01:50,270 --> 00:01:52,980
Step 1: find the cross-section.

28
00:01:53,030 --> 00:01:54,810
Step 2: work out its area.

29
00:01:54,860 --> 00:01:58,110
For a circle, halve the diameter first.

30
00:01:58,160 --> 00:02:01,590
Step 3: multiply by the length or height.

31
00:02:01,640 --> 00:02:05,830
Step 4: give units, and convert to litres if needed.

32
00:02:05,880 --> 00:02:12,720
A triangular prism: the triangle has base 6 and height 4 centimetres, so its area is 12.

33
00:02:12,770 --> 00:02:13,970
The length is 9.

34
00:02:14,020 --> 00:02:17,340
The volume is 108 cubic centimetres.

35
00:02:17,390 --> 00:02:21,510
A can has diameter 8 and height 12 centimetres.

36
00:02:21,560 --> 00:02:28,990
Pi times 16 times 12 is about 603 cubic centimetres, which is 603 millilitres.

37
00:02:29,040 --> 00:02:32,410
A cylinder has volume 500 and radius 5.

38
00:02:32,460 --> 00:02:38,830
The height is 500 divided by 25 pi, which is about 6.4 centimetres.

39
00:02:38,880 --> 00:02:42,780
A cylinder has diameter 10 centimetres and height 4.

40
00:02:42,830 --> 00:02:44,450
Ravi finds the volume.

41
00:02:44,500 --> 00:02:46,520
Which line is wrong?

42
00:02:46,570 --> 00:02:47,620
Line 1 is wrong.

43
00:02:47,670 --> 00:02:49,050
He used the diameter.

44
00:02:49,100 --> 00:02:50,690
The radius is 5.

45
00:02:50,740 --> 00:02:56,290
Pi times 25 times 4 is about 314.2 cubic centimetres.

46
00:02:56,340 --> 00:03:02,490
Engineers use prism and cylinder volumes to size water tanks, pipes and drink cans.

47
00:03:02,540 --> 00:03:03,760
One last question.

48
00:03:03,810 --> 00:03:06,370
A cylinder has radius 1 and height 1.

49
00:03:06,420 --> 00:03:09,110
What is its volume, to 1 decimal place?

50
00:03:09,160 --> 00:03:12,410
Pause the video and answer.

51
00:03:12,460 --> 00:03:16,070
Pi times 1 times 1 is pi, about 3.1.

52
00:03:16,120 --> 00:03:21,440
To remember: prism volume is the cross-section area times the length.

53
00:03:21,490 --> 00:03:23,990
Cylinder: pi R squared H.

54
00:03:24,040 --> 00:03:26,830
1 cubic metre is 1,000 litres.

55
00:03:26,880 --> 00:03:30,710
1,000 cubic centimetres is 1 litre.

56
00:03:30,760 --> 00:03:33,050
Halve the diameter.

57
00:03:33,100 --> 00:03:38,510
Great work! Now practise lesson 14.1 on Camday.
